<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Funan</h1><p class="st-meta">50 CE – 627 CE (approx.) · capital Vyadhapura</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The earliest major state of mainland Southeast Asia known from Chinese records, a Mekong delta power whose port of Oc Eo linked Indian Ocean and China Sea trade.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Funan is known mainly from Chinese envoys and histories from the 3rd century onward, which tell of a foreign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaundinya-i">Kaundinya</a>, who married a local queen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-ye">Liu Ye</a>, and founded the ruling line. Archaeology in the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a>, especially at the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oc-eo">Oc Eo</a> and the hill of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-borei">Angkor Borei</a>, shows canals and trading towns from the 1st century CE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Funan's wealth came from controlling the sea route across the isthmus and around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-thailand">Gulf of Thailand</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oc-eo">Oc Eo</a> has yielded Roman coins, Indian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> and Chinese mirrors. Its kings adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> titles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Buddhist cults and Indian court culture, while a network of canals drained the delta for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>. Chinese sources name rulers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fan-shiman">Fan Shiman</a>, who extended its power by sea.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 6th and early 7th centuries Funan was overtaken by its former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chenla">Chenla</a>, and Chinese records stop mentioning it after the 620s. Its Indianised kingship and religious traditions passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> states that followed, including the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/funan.jpg" alt="Funan" width="320" height="334"><figcaption>Image: Emmanuel de Chambost (France) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_indianized_kingdoms_indochina.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>50 CE – 627 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> – <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vyadhapura</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Fan Shiman, Kaundinya Jayavarman, Rudravarman</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Probably Old Khmer; Sanskrit (inscriptions)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Phù Nam, Nokor Phnom, Bnam</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=338&amp;focus=funan" aria-label="Funan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7549 1706 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Funan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7886 2003h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7869 1962h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7869 1962h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Cambodia. <a href="/atlas?year=338&amp;focus=funan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

